<p><strong>Salon management software runs the daily work of a salon in one system: online bookings by stylist, staff schedules, payments, discounts and reports. For salons with more than one location it also shows every site in one place. This page explains what it is and how a day runs on it &mdash; if you are deciding which one to buy, read the <a href="/blog/best-salon-software-nepal/">salon software buyer's guide</a> instead.</strong></p>

<h2>What salon management software does</h2>
<p>It replaces the paper appointment book, phone calls and chat messages with one calendar. Clients choose a service, a stylist and a time, the slot is held, and the front desk, managers and owner all see the same day. Payments and discounts are recorded against each booking, so the end of the day is a check, not a search.</p>
<p>The word "management" is doing real work in that phrase. A booking tool stops at the calendar. Management software carries the booking through to the money: who delivered the service, what was charged, how it was paid, what was discounted and who approved it.</p>

<h2>A salon day, from opening to close</h2>
<p>This is the part that is hard to picture from a feature list, so here it is in order.</p>

<h3>Opening</h3>
<p>The front desk opens the day's calendar and sees every stylist's column: who is in, who is on leave, who is covering a shift at another branch. Staff mark themselves present. Nobody rings the owner to ask who turned up.</p>

<h3>During the day</h3>
<p>Bookings arrive two ways. Clients book themselves online, choosing a service and a stylist, and the slot is held the moment they confirm. Walk-ins are added by the front desk against a free stylist. Either way the system refuses a clash, so the same stylist cannot be promised to two people at once &mdash; the mistake that costs a salon a client rather than a slot.</p>
<p>As each client leaves, the payment is recorded against their booking by method: cash, card or online. Part-payments are recorded as part-payments. If a stylist wants to give a discount beyond their limit, the request goes to a manager and the approval is recorded with it.</p>

<h3>Closing</h3>
<p>The daily close compares what was booked, what was delivered and what was collected, per branch. Because each payment was recorded as it happened, this is a short check rather than an evening of counting slips and asking people what they remember. Where the numbers disagree, the booking that caused it is named.</p>

<h2>What it looks like across the month</h2>
<p>The daily records add up into the things an owner otherwise reconstructs by hand: revenue by branch, which stylists are busiest and which services earn most, how much went out in discounts and who approved them, and how many hours each staff member actually worked. That last one feeds commission and <a href="/blog/salary-calculation-takes-days-spa-salon-payroll-nepal/">monthly payroll</a>, which is the job most salon owners lose days to.</p>

<h2>One salon or several?</h2>
<p>A single salon can run on a simple calendar. Once you have a second location, the problems change: branches promise the same stylist, prices drift, discounts go unrecorded and the owner cannot see yesterday's numbers without phoning each manager. If you plan to grow, choose software that handles multiple locations from the start. See our guide to <a href="/blog/multi-location-booking-software/">multi-location booking software</a>.</p>

<h2>What it does not do</h2>
<p>Being clear about this saves a wasted demo. Zennly records what was charged and how it was paid against each booking; it is not a POS, and it does not issue tax invoices or handle billing compliance. Outreach to clients is by email as standard, with SMS and WhatsApp available on request rather than switched on by default. There is no Khalti or eSewa integration and no open API today.</p>

<h2>Salon, hair salon or beauty parlour?</h2>
<p>These are the same kind of business. 'Salon' is the common word in the USA, UK, Canada, Australia and the UAE, while 'beauty parlour' is common in Nepal and South Asia. The software works the same way &mdash; though parlours run a different service mix, which is covered in <a href="/blog/beauty-parlour-software-nepal/">beauty parlour software in Nepal</a>.</p>

<h2>Where Zennly fits</h2>
<p>Zennly is built for salons with several branches that need one booking calendar, staff transfers between locations, discount approval and a daily close. If you only need a simple calendar for one chair, a lighter tool may be enough. See <a href="/solutions/salon/">how Zennly works for salons</a>.</p>
